      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-139735",
      ":question/problem": "A company collected data on the sale price (in thousands of dollars) of a particular used car model and the car's age (in years). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price $(y)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8.5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"
