      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-41467",
      ":question/problem": "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"
